INT. MUNICIPAL CLOCK TOWER - MECHANISM ROOM - DAY

A cramped stone chamber crowded with brass gears the size of cartwheels. A pendulum swings slowly behind a wire cage. Dust floats in the shafts of light from a narrow slit window. The ticking is steady, enormous, almost restful.

ARTHUR PENROSE-LIND, 60s, silver mustache, tweed jacket, kneels over a toolbox with the unhurried air of a man being paid by the hour. His partner MIRIAM VOSS, 50s, sharp-eyed and gray-braided, sits on an upturned bucket with a ledger on her knee. DESMOND "DEZ" OYELOWO, 30s, in a maintenance jumpsuit, oils a gear with a long-spouted can while keeping one ear on the stairwell. Perched on the stairs, a nervous teenager, CALLUM BRIGHT, 17, holds a walkie-talkie he has not been asked to use.

ARTHUR
Fine work, Dez. The old girl's never sounded better.

DEZ
She hasn't been oiled since Truman.

MIRIAM
(not looking up)
The bank vault is directly below the east face. Four feet of brick, then the floor.

ARTHUR
And the pendulum shaft goes down how far?

MIRIAM
Right to the ceiling of the vault annex. Nobody ever sealed it.

Arthur nods, pulls a coil of rope from the toolbox, and measures it against his arm. Dez sets down the oil can and checks the cage latch. Callum shifts on the step. The walkie-talkie crackles with static, and he fumbles it to silence.

CALLUM
Sorry.

ARTHUR
Nothing to be sorry for, lad. Nothing at all.

MIRIAM
Thursday, then. After the noon chime, before the council tour.

DEZ
The council tour is at one.

MIRIAM
Then we have forty minutes. Plenty.

The clock gathers itself and strikes the quarter hour. The gears turn. Nobody flinches.
